          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Public Image</strong><br>\nHadrian was known for his relationship with a young Greek, Antinous, who drowned in the Nile under mysterious circumstances. Hadrian deified Antinous, creating a cult that was widespread across the empire. This action and his Hellenophile tendencies shaped his public image, portraying him as a cultured and passionate leader.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Policies</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Hadrian displayed religious tolerance and was interested in various cults and philosophies. He attempted to unify the empire's religious practices by promoting the worship of certain deities and occasionally participating in Jewish and Christian traditions, albeit keeping them under state control.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Succession</strong><br>\nHadrian died on July 10, 138 AD, after a long illness. His reign ended with him choosing Antoninus Pius as his successor, ensuring a peaceful transition of power. Hadrian left behind a mixed legacy marked by his efforts to solidify the empire, patronage of the arts, and reformation of Roman laws.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
